    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d598">

                <pb facs="0495" n="483" sameAs="frus:frus1942v04/pg483"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00112 European War 1939/6669: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State to the Chargé at
                        Tangier</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Childs</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1942-10-15T22:00:00-04:00">October 15,
                            1942—10 p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>187. Your despatch 1015, August 31.<anchor n="54" xml:id="d598-f1"/> The Board of
                    Economic Warfare has lately taken the position that no export licenses should be
                    issued for supplies for Spanish North Africa pending the conclusion of the
                    proposed economic accord. Your view as expressed in your despatch seems to favor
                    the issuance of export licenses and navicerts on a limited basis until the
                    accord is concluded. The Board of Economic Warfare desires to know what your
                    present views are on the subject before considering a possible relaxation of its
                    present policy.</p>
                <p>What is the status at the moment of the negotiations with Orgaz?</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Welles</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
